  "markdown_report": "The best first experiment is **matched history-deletion consistency for SASRec**, supported by architecture-aware perturbation diagnostics. Progress is 0%, so a small, reversible intervention is preferable to introducing an unvalidated compatibility model. No improvement has yet been measured.\n\n**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Training-position margins measure discrimination against sampled negatives; they do not establish final-position ranking quality. Diagnose the final nonpadding representation separately. (2) Qualitative feedback suggests excessive influence from isolated historical interests. This motivates controlled deletion experiments, but does not establish that these interactions caused ranking errors. (3) Metadata and interaction histories cannot establish ownership, satisfaction, or technical compatibility. Missing evidence must remain unknown, and unavailable category breakdowns must stay empty.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) Scarcelli et al. and TRACE motivate measuring recommendation changes under history edits; sensitivity demonstrates model dependence, not changed user preference [1,2]. (2) Petrov et al. motivate separating last-item dependence from earlier ordering: compare unrestricted shuffling with shuffling that preserves the final item [3]. (3) Sampling-reliability research motivates keeping benchmark selection distinct from full-catalog stress tests; their rankings need not agree [4].\n\n**Directions and framework.** Organize experiments along three axes: history dependence, ranking discrimination, and evidence grounding. Their respective controls are count/age-matched perturbations, fixed candidate sets, and supported/contradicted/unknown textual evidence. The missing connection is whether a measured behavioral weakness identifies an intervention that improves macro validation HR@10.\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality / future potential / code difficulty, each out of 10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n- **Matched deletion consistency — 5/7/4.** Preserve rankings after deleting one old, semantically isolated event. Positive: directly connects a diagnostic hypothesis to an inexpensive intervention. Negative: consistency can suppress legitimate long-tail interests [1,2].\n- **Recency residual scorer — 3/6/3.** Add a learned mixture of SASRec scores and recency-weighted item similarity. Positive: provides a simple test of recency benefits. Negative: may intensify already excessive last-item dependence [3].\n- **Evidence-grounded compatibility audit — 5/6/6.** Extract prerequisite claims with exact title/description spans and abstain when unsupported. Positive: makes qualitative errors auditable. Negative: incomplete specifications limit coverage and provide no direct ranking improvement [5].\n\n**Selected algorithm.** Build sparse TF–IDF title/description vectors using training-history items. Flag older events with low similarity to other history events; retain the final interaction. Compare their deletion effects with repeated count- and age-matched random deletions. If excess sensitivity appears, train a small consistency-weight sweep, retaining zero as the baseline. Apply consistency only to the final valid training target, preserving ordinary SASRec supervision elsewhere.\n\nKeep all four routes, seeds, splits and pinned evaluation unchanged. Select by equal-weight macro validation HR@10 and report every category. Preserve the diagnostic interface, restore model mode, and mark empty measurements unavailable. Use bounded cohorts and cached vectors within the shared GPU budget; interpret semantic isolation only as a proxy.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a small final-position consistency penalty to SASRec when removing an older, semantically isolated history event, after checking sensitivity against count- and age-matched random deletions.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback raises the hypothesis that isolated historical interactions exert excessive influence. Existing margin diagnostics cannot test this hypothesis. Controlled perturbations connect observable history dependence to a minimal training intervention without inferring ownership, dislikes, or simulator traits.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], all four dataset routes, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and the environment-controlled 200-epoch default. Fit sparse TF–IDF on titles/descriptions of training-history items only; missing text makes semantic isolation unavailable. Define an eligible event as preceding the final history event and lying in the older half of the prefix. Its isolation score is one minus its maximum cosine similarity to another prefix event. Compare deletion of high-isolation events with repeated random eligible deletions matched by age bin, using identical candidates and eligibility masks. Report compacted deletion separately from position-preserving masking. Use detached clean candidate distributions as consistency targets; tune a small penalty-weight set including zero using pinned macro validation HR@10. Sampled non-target items remain unlabeled alternatives. No inference architecture change is required, but verify the existing predictor writer exports the selected trained checkpoint. DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader) must return metrics and metric_definitions; diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis) must embed both. Use view.model for evaluation-mode changes and restore its prior mode in a finally block. Add valid-sample counts, final-position margins, top-10 overlap, and perturbation rank displacement with explicit denominators. Empty cohorts produce unavailable values rather than zeros. Never populate unavailable category, review, commercial-attribute, or trait fields.",
    "pseudocode": "Fit training-only sparse text vectors; cache eligible deletion positions.\nOn a bounded diagnostic cohort:\n    score original histories on fixed candidates\n    compare isolated-event deletion with repeated age/count-matched deletions\n    record top-10 overlap and original-item rank displacement\nIf isolated-event sensitivity exceeds matched controls:\n    for lambda in a small set including 0:\n        initialize SASRec with seed 42\n        for each training batch:\n            base_loss = ordinary SASRec supervised loss\n            select examples with an eligible older event\n            compact each selected prefix after one deletion; retain its final event\n            C = shared valid target plus sampled candidate IDs\n            p = softmax(clean_final_scores[C]).detach()\n            q = softmax(edited_final_scores[C])\n            loss = base_loss + lambda * mean(KL(p || q))\n            optimize(loss)\n        evaluate all four categories using the pinned validation protocol\nSelect the checkpoint by equal-weight macro HR@10, including the unchanged baseline.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Combines matched perturbation controls with a narrowly targeted consistency intervention tied to the observed qualitative failure hypothesis.",
      "negative": "History augmentation and consistency regularization are established techniques; the contribution is their controlled targeting and evaluation."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Can test whether behavioral diagnostics predict useful interventions across heterogeneous recommendation domains.",
      "negative": "Semantic isolation may identify legitimate niche interests, and perturbation stability may not improve next-item accuracy."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Reuses SASRec, adds sparse text preprocessing and an edited-prefix forward pass, and leaves inference unchanged.",
      "negative": "Correct prefix compaction, target alignment, matched controls, and bounded diagnostic costs require careful implementation."
    }
  },
